    "body": "## Summary\n\nAdds **ADR-032: Visibility + Opacity Paint Support** - a small structural follow-up chosen after ADR-031 shipped with a suspected paint overlap.\n\n**Documentation only.** Implementation (~40-60 lines) lands in a follow-up PR.\n\n## Honest scope call\n\n**This ADR's visible delta on Wikipedia today is near-zero.** Measurement shows:\n- `visibility`: 2 uses (1 visible, 1 hidden -- hidden one is main-menu, already handled by ADR-029)\n- `opacity`: 23 uses (5 raw `1`, 4 raw `0`, 14 `var(--opacity-*)` references)\n\nIts value is structural:\n1. **Precondition for ADR-033 (`:checked` dropdowns)** - 14 `:checked` rules use `visibility:visible` to reveal dropdown content on click. Initial hidden state needs paint culling to work\n2. **May or may not fix ADR-031's apparent overlap** - depends on whether the overlap is a paint bug (drawing `display:none` subtree layout skipped) or stale render\n\n## What ADR-031's leak_locate diagnostic showed\n\nAll suspicious fragments (`HTML5` / `text/html` / `WHATWG` / `public.html`) are at:\n- **Left side (x<500)**: 54 fragments, all at **y >= 4317** (below first viewport)\n- **Right side (x>=500)**: 18 fragments at correct infobox positions (y >= 523)\n- **Zero fragments in the overlap zone** (first viewport, left column)\n\nSo the \"overlap\" seen in the earlier PNG either was stale, or is a paint-layer issue that doesn't show in the layout tree. `visibility`/`opacity` culling can't remove something layout already skipped -- but it CAN fix a paint-layer bug if that's the cause.\n\n## Decision\n\nParse `visibility` and `opacity` into a paint-time `visible` bool on `LayoutBox`, set during layout, respected by paint:\n\n- `visibility: hidden | collapse` → `visible = false`\n- `opacity: 0` → `visible = false`\n- `visibility: visible` on hidden-ancestor descendant → **not re-enabled in v1** (documented limitation; only 1 use in Wikipedia's CSS, already worked around)\n- `opacity: var(...)` → treated as opaque (no var resolution in v1)\n- `opacity: 0.0001..1` → treated as opaque (no alpha blending in v1)\n\nPaint early-returns for `visible == false` subtrees.\n\n## Two honest caveats\n\n1. **May not fix ADR-031's overlap.** If the overlap persists after this ADR, the cause is deeper (stale render, paint traversal bug, or `shift_subtree_x` recursion) and gets its own diagnostic cycle.\n\n2. **v1 has no opacity blending.** Any opacity > 0 is drawn opaque. Blending (Cairo groups or per-draw alpha) deferred to a future ADR. In Wikipedia's CSS, `opacity` is used for button states, hover transitions, and modal overlays -- none in the first viewport without user interaction.\n\n## Test plan\n\nImplementation will add 6 tests in a new `[paint][adr-032]` tag:\n- `visibility:hidden` subtree not painted\n- `visibility:visible` subtree painted\n- `opacity:0` subtree not painted\n- `opacity:0.5` subtree painted (v1: opaque)\n- `opacity:var(--x)` painted (var doesn't resolve)\n- default (no visibility/opacity) painted\n\n## Expected visible delta after implementation\n\n**On Wikipedia today**: near-zero. The 2 visibility uses are already handled. The overlap (if real) will either fix or persist -- both outcomes documented in ADR-031's Post-ADR-032 note.\n\n**Structural**: paint becomes authoritative about visibility. Unblocks ADR-033 (`:checked` dropdowns), which will be the first ADR to produce a real positive delta using this culling path.\n\nRefs #171\n",
